  "discussions_data": [
    {
      "id": "660d99b244c8880d7ceeade824d687fa22c3cb38",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 426254295,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Why would an octet string necessarily be ascii printable?  I guess in the originally raised case the type has been \"abused\" to actually hold an ASCII string representing a timestamp, but is that true in the general case?",
          "author": {
            "id": 6621762,
            "username": "graham.bloice",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Graham Bloice",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/8730bfac2267cb97ff1f8187d285f244745a03a783055c8fd6fcd679bfa71c6e?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/graham.bloice"
          },
          "created_at": "2020-10-08T13:47:27.384Z",
          "updated_at": "2020-10-08T13:47:27.384Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 73722897,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 533,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1d168da083ad78de9e135a6b08bdbce9ed6648d3",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 426348166,
          "type": null,
          "body": "No an octet string isn't necessarily printable but what BASE_SHOW_ASCII_PRINTABLE is doing is checking if it is and if it is then print it as a string. In this case I think it will\nmake the dissection better in the case where no MIB is loaded as well.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6792385,
            "username": "AndersBroman",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "AndersBroman",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/132fb48f26018ea9516aea909144af3c3a94eaf2ba477efaa9a232abe80d322c?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/AndersBroman"
          },
          "created_at": "2020-10-08T15:18:06.318Z",
          "updated_at": "2020-10-08T15:18:06.318Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 73722897,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 533,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "245a7c42f8c0b90a4166a97c65cfc3f14cc63199",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": true,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 426372734,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "OK, so if it isn't ASCII, then the hex is displayed as before?\n\nDoes the check comprise all octets, or just a portion before deciding it's printable?\n\nWhat I'm hoping to avoid is less info being displayed than before when it really isn't an ASCII string but just happens to have values in the printable range.\n\nFWIW, whichever method is employed, the actual byes, both hex and ASCII (if printable) are available in the packet bytes pane, but that won't be there (normally) on tshark output.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6621762,
            "username": "graham.bloice",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Graham Bloice",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/8730bfac2267cb97ff1f8187d285f244745a03a783055c8fd6fcd679bfa71c6e?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/graham.bloice"
          },
          "created_at": "2020-10-08T15:50:31.128Z",
          "updated_at": "2020-10-09T06:31:49.475Z",
